score":0.2,"confidence":"low","evidence":["catalog text contains \"quest\""]},{"id":"accessible","score":0.18,"confidence":"low","evidence":["catalog text contains \"popular\""]}],"method":"deterministic_catalog_heuristics_v1"},"experimentalSemanticProfile":{"centralFigures":[],"centralPlaces":[],"argument":{"present":true,"statement":"Claims about future war are often wrong because they idealize it as confined, brief, and decisive while underestimating the likelihood of long wars and the blurred boundaries among military, civilian, and criminal violence.","confidence":0.66},"academicOrientation":{"score":38,"confidence":0.45},"profileConfidence":0.74,"model":"gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"dfbe86f2cbcfaf9dccee18015f101c69114495cc5572df124ca73b235b128a6d","reviewStatus":"unreviewed","validationWarnings":[]},"primarySubject":"War & Military","secondarySubjects":["History","Politics & Government"],"confidence":"high","rationale":"The book primarily examines the evolution and future of warfare, framed within military history and its political implications, making 'War & Military' the dominant subject.","method":"llm","model":"gpt-4o-mini","reviewStatus":"generated","inputHash":"c67fc9c218052b0e0f2801124bb65bac2d1023e0a2d45e397bad4a351400a636","summary":"Questions about the future of war are a regular feature of political debate, strategic analysis, and popular fiction. Where should we look for new dangers? What cunning plans might an aggressor have in mind? What are the best forms of defense? How might peace be preserved or conflict resolved? From the French rout at Sedan in 1870 to the relentless contemporary insurgencies in Iraq and Afghanistan, Lawrence Freedman, a world-renowned military thinker, reveals how most claims from the military futurists are wrong. But they remain influential nonetheless. Freedman shows how those who have imagined future war have often had an idealized notion of it as confined, brief, and decisive, and have regularly taken insufficient account of the possibility of long wars--hence the stubborn persistence of the idea of a knockout blow, whether through a dashing land offensive, nuclear first strike, or cyberattack. He also notes the lack of attention paid to civil wars until the West began to intervene in them during the 1990s, and how the boundaries between peace and war, between the military, the civilian, and the criminal are becoming increasingly blurred. Freedman's account of a century and a half of warfare and the (often misconceived) thinking that precedes war is a challenge to hawks and doves alike, and puts current strategic thinking into a bracing historical perspective.","pageCount":376,"publicationYear":2017,"subjectCategories":[{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Forecasting","sourceId":"source-open-library-the-future-of-war-a-history-lawrence-freedman"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"War","sourceId":"source-open-library-the-future-of-war-a-history-lawrence-freedman"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"History","sourceId":"source-open-library-the-future-of-war-a-history-lawrence-freedman"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"War, history","sourceId":"source-open-library-the-future-of-war-a-history-lawrence-freedman"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"New York Times reviewed","sourceId":"source-open-library-the-future-of-war-a-history-lawrence-freedman"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"SOCIAL SCIENCE / Future Studies","sourceId":"source-open-library-the-future-of-war-a-history-lawrence-freedman"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"POLITICAL SCIENCE / Security (National & International)","sourceId":"source-open-library-the-future-of-war-a-history-lawrence-freedman"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"HISTORY / Military / Strategy","sourceId":"source-open-library-the-future-of-war-a-history-lawrence-freedman"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"War--forecasting","sourceId":"source-open-library-the-future-of-war-a-history-lawrence-freedman"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"War--history","sourceId":"source-open-library-the-future-of-war-a-history-lawrence-freedman"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"U21.2 .f74 2017","sourceId":"source-open-library-the-future-of-war-a-history-lawrence-freedman"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"355.02","sourceId":"source-open-library-the-future-of-war-a-history-lawrence-freedman"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"POLITICAL SCIENCE / International Relations","sourceId":"source-open-library-the-future-of-war-a-history-lawrence-freedman"}],"displaySummary":"From the French rout at Sedan in 1870 to the relentless contemporary insurgencies in Iraq and Afghanistan, Lawrence Freedman, a world-renowned military thinker, reveals how most claims from the military futurists are wrong. But they remain influential nonetheless. Freedman shows how those who have imagined future war have often had an idealized notion of it as confined, brief, and decisive, and have regularly taken insufficient account of the possibility of long wars--hence the stubborn persistence of the idea of a knockout blow, whether through a dashing land offensive, nuclear first strike, or cyberattack. He also notes the lack of attention paid to civil wars until the West began to intervene in them during the 1990s, and how the boundaries between peace and war, between the military, the civilian, and the criminal are becoming increasingly blurred. Freedman's account of a century and a half of warfare and the (often misconceived) thinking that precedes war is a challenge to hawks and doves alike, and puts current strategic thinking